## Solution

The robot rolls a fair six-sided die repeatedly, accumulating a running total $S$. It stops as soon as $S > 20$. The final total must therefore lie in $\{21, 22, 23, 24, 25, 26\}$, since the last roll adds between 1 and 6 to a total that was $\le 20$.

**Setup.** Let $g(s)$ denote the probability that the running total ever equals exactly $s$ (with $s \le 20$, so the robot continues). Then $g(0)=1$, and for $s\ge 1$:

$$g(s) = \frac{1}{6}\sum_{r=1}^{6} g(s-r), \qquad g(n)=0 \text{ for } n<0.$$

The probability that the final total equals $k$ (for $k\in\{21,\dots,26\}$) is:

$$P(k) = \frac{1}{6}\sum_{s=k-6}^{20} g(s),$$

since the robot must be at some state $s\le 20$ and then roll exactly $k-s \in \{1,\dots,6\}$.

**Computing $g(s)$.** For $s\le 6$, the sum includes all of $g(0),\dots,g(s-1)$, giving the closed form $g(s) = 7^{s-1}/6^s$ for $s\ge 1$ (verified by induction using the geometric sum). For $s>6$, the six-term recurrence applies. Computing numerically through $s=20$:

| $s$ | $g(s)$ | | $s$ | $g(s)$ |
|-----|--------|---|-----|--------|
| 0 | 1.0000 | | 11 | 0.2934 |
| 1 | 0.1667 | | 12 | 0.2908 |
| 2 | 0.1944 | | 13 | 0.2793 |
| 3 | 0.2269 | | 14 | 0.2835 |
| 4 | 0.2647 | | 15 | 0.2861 |
| 5 | 0.3088 | | 16 | 0.2871 |
| 6 | 0.3602 | | 17 | 0.2867 |
| 7 | 0.2536 | | 18 | 0.2856 |
| 8 | 0.2681 | | 19 | 0.2847 |
| 9 | 0.2804 | | 20 | 0.2856 |
| 10 | 0.2893 | | | |

**Final probabilities.**

| $k$ | $P(k)$ |
|-----|--------|
| 21 | $\frac{1}{6}(g(15)+\cdots+g(20)) \approx 0.2860$ |
| 22 | $\frac{1}{6}(g(16)+\cdots+g(20)) \approx 0.2383$ |
| 23 | $\frac{1}{6}(g(17)+\cdots+g(20)) \approx 0.1904$ |
| 24 | $\frac{1}{6}(g(18)+g(19)+g(20)) \approx 0.1427$ |
| 25 | $\frac{1}{6}(g(19)+g(20)) \approx 0.0951$ |
| 26 | $\frac{1}{6}g(20) \approx 0.0476$ |

These sum to $\approx 1.0000$, confirming correctness.

**Why 21 wins.** The values $g(15),\dots,g(20)$ are all nearly equal ($\approx 0.285$–$0.287$), since the distribution of the running total near 20 has stabilized. The probability $P(k)$ is proportional to the *number* of starting states $s\in\{k-6,\dots,20\}$ that can reach $k$, weighted by roughly equal $g(s)$ values. The final total $21$ can be reached from all six states $\{15,16,17,18,19,20\}$, while each larger $k$ loses one starting state. Hence $P(21) > P(22) > \cdots > P(26)$, strictly decreasing.

**The most probable final total distance is $\boxed{21}$.**

